just about every state and local  government is in a financial bind  because they're spending more than they  take in public sector pensions are a  major factor here  they already represent a big part of  spending and they're underfunded by  somewhere between one and five trillion  dollars as those shortfalls come do  either taxpayers are going to get  screwed through higher taxes and reduce  services where public sector retirees  are going to take a hell of a haircut  here are three reasons to fix public  sector pensions now  unsustainable pension payouts played a  large role at municipal bankruptcies in  places such as Detroit and Vallejo  California in the 250 largest cities in  the country pensions on average soak up  10% of budgets a 30 percent increase  over just the past five years as  retirees start outnumbering workers and  the new york police department already  has more former cops drawing a pension  than active ones walking a beat the  amount of money spent on pensions is  only get a skyrocket  public employees typically get to retire  earlier and they pay less into their  retirements than their counterparts in  the private sector on top of that many  workers gain the system through often  legal tricks such as spiking which is  the act of artificially boosting the  salary level upon which the retirement  payments are calculated for instance by  cashing in unused sick days of vacation  Phoenix Arizona's former city manager  was able to jack up his annual  retirement pay by almost $90,000 to a  whopping two hundred thirty four  thousand five hundred and thirty six  dollars  most government pensions guaranteed  payouts based on unrealistic investment  returns California said the investments  in its pension funds would return 8%  every year while the actual returns have  been far lower the results is that  California's pension fund are only about  42 percent funded taxpayers ultimately  pick up the difference as more public  sector workers retire pensions are only  going to become a bigger and bigger  issue it's time to reform public sector  pensions by reining in lavish promises  closing loopholes and switching over to  pay-as-you-go 401k style plans that most  private sector workers have for reason  TV I'm Nicholas
 

